[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | environment | `LC_ALL=C`, `en_US.UTF-8`, `tr_TR.UTF-8`, `LANG=de_DE.UTF-8`, `LC_NUMERIC=de_DE.UTF-8` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdout | pipe drained, pipe with a dead reader, regular file, `/dev/null`, `/dev/full` (every write fails ENOSPC), descriptor closed (EBADF), pseudo-terminal, merged with stderr |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — exit status on a broken stdout pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout on a pipe whose read end was already closed, the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program exited with status 141 while the Rust program exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m phase_c_stdout_is_a_pipe_whose_reader_is_already_gone
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   left:  (Some(141), None)     # C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   right: (Some(0),   None)     # Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` during runtime
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m startup, before `main` is entered. A C program inherits the default disposition.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So a failing write made the C program die from signal 13 — shell status 141 —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whereas in Rust the write merely returned `EPIPE`, which the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discarded (matching C's habit of ignoring `printf`'s return value), after which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` fell through to `exit(0)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the C starting state with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, before any output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is produced. `signal` and `isatty` are declared in a small `extern "C"` block so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the crate keeps its dependency-free `Cargo.toml`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified out of band as well as in the suite: with the read end of a pipe closed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before launch, both binaries now report `returncode=-13`, i.e. killed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGPIPE`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — stdout buffering discipline
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Not a difference in bytes, but a difference in *when* those bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reach the descriptor, which changed the outcome of the broken-pipe race. Running
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `prog | true` twenty times, the failures were one-directional: C reported 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m while Rust reported 0, never the reverse.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The original translation wrote through `std::io::stdout()`, which is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a `LineWriter` regardless of what stdout is connected to. That produced seven
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m separate writes, the first of them almost immediately after startup. C stdio
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead picks its mode from the descriptor: line buffered when `isatty(1)`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fully buffered otherwise. Against a pipe, the C program accumulated all 106
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes and emitted them in a single write during exit-time flush. Rust was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m therefore writing early and often where C wrote once and late, giving the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m programs systematically different exposure to a pipe that was in the process of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `print_line` now appends into a process-wide buffer and flushes it the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m way C stdio would — immediately when `isatty(1)`, otherwise only once 4096 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m have accumulated (glibc sizes a fully buffered stream from the descriptor's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `st_blksize`) or at exit. The flush writes straight to descriptor 1 through a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ManuallyDrop<File>` so it lands in one `write` call, and write errors are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discarded exactly as the C code discards `printf`'s return value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Result.** The remaining `prog | true` divergences became symmetric — both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c=141 r=0` and `c=0 r=141` now occur, at roughly equal rates — which is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signature of an inherent scheduling race between two separately launched
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m processes rather than a behavioural difference. The deterministic form of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same scenario, where the reader is confirmed dead before launch, agrees on every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run and is what the test suite asserts. The racy shell pipeline is deliberately
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not asserted on.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Checks that found no mismatch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Recorded so the next reader knows these were actually exercised, not assumed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Exact transcript: seven lines, one `\n` each, no trailing blank line, no `\r`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   no leading or trailing spaces — `printf("%s\n", line)` with no field width.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `helperBad()` never appears on stdout, and `helperGood()` appears exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   once. A translation that "tidied up" `bad()` to call its unused helper, by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   symmetry with `good()`, would be caught here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stderr is empty in every case, and the exit status is 0 in every case that is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   not a broken pipe.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - argv is never inspected: 2000 arguments, a 64 KiB argument, an argument that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is not valid UTF-8, and an empty-string argument all yield the same bytes. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   non-UTF-8 case matters because a `char *` carries no encoding guarantee, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   translation that eagerly converted `args()` to `String` would panic.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdin is never read: 1 MiB of unread input does not deadlock or truncate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   either program, and a closed descriptor 0 changes nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Locale does not reach `printf` here — no `setlocale` call, and no numeric
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   conversions in the format string.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Failing writes are ignored identically. Against `/dev/full` every write fails
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   with `ENOSPC`, and against a closed descriptor 1 with `EBADF`; both programs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stay silent on stderr and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - On a pseudo-terminal both produce the same 99 bytes, the terminal driver's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ONLCR` having expanded each `\n` to `\r\n` for both equally. This is the case
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   that exercises the line-buffered branch of the buffering fix.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Output is byte-identical across repeated runs; nothing depends on a pointer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   value, a hash seed or a timestamp.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Unreachable in the C source, and left unreachable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printLine(NULL)` and `helperBad()` cannot be triggered through any input, so no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m test drives them. The Rust translation keeps both shapes — `Option<&str>` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m its `None` arm, and `helper_bad` behind `#[allow(dead_code)]` — so the structure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m still corresponds to the C, but neither can affect observable behaviour.
